  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Constructing the React App**\n\nI\'m currently focused on building the React/Vite application. The goal is a single-page app, integrating UI design and style guide elements. We\'re prioritizing a single-view structure with client-side routing.\n\n\n**Defining the Application Screens**\n\nI\'ve realized the single-view constraint needs careful interpretation, given multiple provided HTML examples. The user\'s request, I believe, necessitates using a single-page React app that dynamically renders different \"screens\" (Dashboard, Gallery, Profiles, Live Stream, and Landing Page) based on a state variable. I am now considering the component structure to handle these screens effectively. The Tailwind CSS config looks promising.\n\n\n**Structuring the Application Screens**\n\nI\'m now diving into the component structure, starting with `App.tsx` for routing and layout, controlled by an `activeTab` state. I\'ll define `Navigation.tsx`, and screen components (`DashboardScreen`, `GalleryScreen`, etc.). My focus is blending the provided HTML examples into a unified SPA with either top or side navigation, adapting to the \"screens\" requested. I\'m focusing on the Landing Page\'s structure as a key element.\n\n\n**Refining the Screen Structure**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on managing distinct views within the React application. The strategy involves a `currentView` state in `App.tsx` and dedicated components for each screen: landing, dashboard, gallery, profiles, and live stream. I am carefully considering the most effective way to address the user\'s provided HTML blocks. I\'m aiming for modular code to minimize token usage. I\'m focusing on the conversion of the provided Tailwind config, aiming to use Tailwind classes directly, or adapt it to the project\'s CSS with `@theme` directives, since the project uses `@tailwindcss/vite`. I plan to start with `src/index.css` to define the styling.\n\n\n**Implementing the Tailwind Theme**\n\nI\'m now integrating the provided design tokens into the project\'s Tailwind configuration. I\'m focusing on defining these custom properties within `src/index.css` using `@theme` directives. This will allow for easy access and consistent styling throughout the application.\n\n\n**Defining the CSS Variables**\n\nI\'m now focused on the Tailwind configuration, integrating the provided design tokens, defining custom properties. I\'ll use `src/index.css` with `@theme` directives for global access and consistent styling. The goal is easy access across the application.\n\n\n**Integrating New Design Tokens**\n\nI\'m now incorporating additional design tokens. I\'ve focused on adding new font families (Epilogue, Be Vietnam Pro), and spacing variables (margin, container, section gap, unit, gutter, margin mobile). I am also adding utility classes. I\'ll test these new utility classes by updating the style examples.\n\n\n\n**Revising Tailwind Integration**\n\nI\'m now revising my approach to Tailwind integration. I\'ve realized `tailwind v4` needs exact keys or it is better to just write the `--color-*` variables in `@theme`. I\'ll update `src/index.css`.\n\nI\'m also working on creating the main layouts and navigation, considering that the Top Navigation is consistent across most screens, while the Dashboard uses a Side Navigation. I\'ll implement a unified app structure with a `Navbar` and conditional rendering based on a `currentScreen` state. I have broken down the components into: `Navbar.tsx`, `Sidebar.tsx`, and `MobileNav.tsx`. The screens will be: `LandingScreen.tsx`, `DashboardScreen.tsx`, `GalleryScreen.tsx`, `ProfileScreen.tsx`, and `LiveStreamScreen.tsx`.\nI have also detailed the content needed in each screen.\n\n\n**Updating the CSS Import**\n\nI\'m now changing my focus to Tailwind configurations, aiming for clean component-level CSS. I\'m verifying the current state of `src/index.css`. Next, I\'ll update the index file. I\'ll read its contents, and then replace it with new `@theme` definitions. This helps ensure consistent styling across the application. I\'ll also start replacing the placeholder images and icons from the prompt\'s HTML.\n\n\n**Refining the HTML Structure**\n\nI\'m now focusing on modifying `index.html` to integrate external resources. I\'ve added `<link>` tags for Google Fonts (Epilogue, Be Vietnam Pro) and Material Symbols Outlined. This will ensure the application utilizes the specified fonts and icon library, aligning with the design requirements. I\'ve also verified this will work with the root file.\n\n\n**Updating the CSS Configuration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the provided CSS content into `src/index.css` via a theme. I\'ve focused on using the new design tokens by defining custom properties within `@theme` directives to ensure consistent styling throughout the application. I will be replacing the current placeholder definitions with the provided configurations. I plan to start replacing the design token values for all properties.\n\n\n**Integrating Color Properties**\n\nI\'m now integrating the remaining color properties. I\'ve focused on adding all the colors provided into the `src/index.css` file using the `--color-*` variables defined. These new additions expand the theme.\n\n\n**Adding More Design Tokens**\n\nI\'m now expanding the application\'s theme. I am adding new color properties and font families, integrating them directly into the `src/index.css` file. I\'ll define these new color variables using `--color-*` and new font families to enhance the design system\'s consistency and visual appeal. I\'ll add the new values to the `src/index.css`.\n\n\n**Implementing Utility Classes**\n\nI\'m now implementing the provided utility classes in `src/index.css`. I\'ve added definitions for `grain-bg`, `grain-overlay`, `coral-glow`, and `coral-glow-strong`, along with the `pulse-red` keyframe animation. I plan to use these utility classes to update the style examples.\n\n\n\n**Adding Final Utility Classes**\n\nI\'m now implementing the final utility classes in `src/index.css`. This includes the animation for `.map-pulse`, the new `.tropical-bg`, `.hero-glass`, `.glass-panel`, `.glass-card` classes and `.map-3d-effect`. These updates will allow us to incorporate the provided styles and effects, improving the application\'s overall look. I\'ll test these final classes to ensure they function as intended.\n\nI\'m now implementing the provided utility classes in `src/index.css`. I\'ve added definitions for `grain-bg`, `grain-overlay`, `coral-glow`, and `coral-glow-strong`, along with the `pulse-red` keyframe animation. I plan to use these utility classes to update the style examples.\n\n\n**Organizing the File Structure**\n\nI\'m now establishing the file and directory structure. I\'m placing screen components into `/src/screens` and shared components in `/src/components`. I\'m creating files for `LandingScreen.tsx`, `DashboardScreen.tsx`, `GalleryScreen.tsx`, `ProfileScreen.tsx`, and `LiveStreamScreen.tsx` within `/src/screens`. I\'m also adding `Navigation.tsx`, `Sidebar.tsx`, `MobileNav.tsx` in `/src/components`, and also `types.ts`. I plan to use these components in `App.tsx` using a simple state router for navigation, since `tailwind@4` supports `@theme`.\n\n\n**Structuring the App.tsx**\n\nI am now focusing on structuring `src/App.tsx`. I\'ve added state management for screen navigation, defining `ScreenType` for clarity. I\'m implementing conditional rendering to display the relevant screen components (`LandingScreen`, `DashboardScreen`, `GalleryScreen`, `ProfileScreen`, and `LiveStreamScreen`) based on `currentScreen`.\n\n\n**Refining the Component Structure**\n\nI\'m now revising the component architecture. Considering the provided HTML for each screen, I believe it\'s best if the navigation is handled directly within each screen component. This allows for specific variations in the HTML while minimizing file size. I will organize the screens with separate `.tsx` files, and I\'ll include the raw HTML code for each screen.\n\n\n**Constructing the Landing Screen**\n\nI\'m now focusing on creating the individual screen components. I\'ll define `LandingScreen.tsx` with the structure shown, including `useEffect`. I\'ll replace the `#` hrefs with `onClick` handlers. I\'ll also reuse image `src` tags directly from the HTML, which simplifies integration. I will define `edit_file` and `create_file` now. I\'ll create the five screen files in `/src/screens/` and rewrite `src/App.tsx`.\n\n\n**Mapping the LiveStream Screen**\n\nI\'m now mapping the JSX structure for `LiveStreamScreen.tsx`, handling HTML conversions like `class` to `className` and self-closing tags, and transforming inline styles. I\'m focusing on adding `onClick` handlers for page navigation in the Landing Page navbar, now that it\'s rendered, and I will be using `onNavigate` callbacks to change screens.\n\n\n**Implementing the Side Navigation**\n\nI\'m now implementing `DashboardScreen.tsx`, including the side navigation and main dashboard content. I\'ll translate the HTML structure, and add navigation links using `onClick` handlers. I\'m parallelizing the creation of `App.tsx`, `index.css`, `index.html`, `LandingScreen.tsx`, `DashboardScreen.tsx`, and `GalleryScreen.tsx` to handle the complexity and potential token limitations.\n\n\n**Finalizing the App Screens**\n\nI\'m now integrating the `ProfileScreen.tsx` and `LiveStreamScreen.tsx` components into `App.tsx`, completing the screen integration. I\'m ensuring all navigation links work correctly by using `onNavigate`. I will perform final testing across all screens to verify seamless navigation and proper rendering.\n\n\n\n**Generating Screen Components**\n\nI\'m now generating all screen files concurrently to ensure immediate compilation success, focusing on JSX transformations and inline style adaptation. I\'ve prepared strings for `LandingScreen.tsx`, `DashboardScreen.tsx`, and will quickly create empty placeholders for `ProfileScreen.tsx` and `LiveStreamScreen.tsx`, and then start adding the logic and transforming each screen\'s HTML into working React components. I\'ll test each screen to ensure smooth navigation.\n\n\n**Refactoring the Style Integrations**\n\nI\'m now focusing on removing all remaining inline styles and converting them to Tailwind classes, specifically in `DashboardScreen`, `GalleryScreen`, `ProfileScreen`, and `LiveStreamScreen`. This is to enhance maintainability and adhere to best practices for the project. I\'ll transform inline styles like width and animation delays into their Tailwind class equivalents.\n\n\n**Adapting the HTML for React**\n\nI\'m translating the provided HTML into React components, carefully handling tag conversions and self-closing tags, focusing on replacing `class` with `className` and ensuring proper tag closure. I\'m verifying the HTML for each screen is self-closing and well-formed. This strategy ensures seamless integration with React while preserving the original markup. The images and inputs look pretty standard.\n\n\n**Refining the Tool Calls**\n\nI\'m now revising the tool call strategy to optimize token usage and manage file size constraints. Instead of a single large batch, I\'ll use smaller batches to avoid exceeding token limits during output. I\'ll focus on creating the main application files, ensuring a solid foundation before expanding to the other screen components. I\'ll separate the larger file updates across turns, allowing the model to handle larger payloads, reducing the risk of truncation, and improving the overall integration process.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Component Screens**\n\nI\'m now in the process of generating the `LandingScreen`, `DashboardScreen`, and `GalleryScreen` components, fully translating HTML to JSX, including replacing `class` with `className` and converting inline styles to React compatible format, focusing on clear conversions. I\'m also preparing dummy files for `ProfileScreen` and `LiveStreamScreen` to e