      nanos: 145142314
    }
  }
  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Investigating Property Error**\n\nI\'m currently trying to understand a perplexing \"getter\" error related to the \'fetch\' property. It seems I may need to redefine a property with `Object.defineProperty` due to a conflict with `window.fetch`. I\'m going to check the `vite.config.ts` and `index.html` files.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Mystery Error**\n\nThe \"fetch\" error still confounds me, but the most recent line of thinking points toward environmental issues. I checked the `<script>` tag in `index.html` and examined potential conflicts with `react-dom` and `Lucide React`, which are part of the standard boilerplate. I can\'t find a `fetch` query. Could this be a problem with the AI studio?\n\n\n**Unraveling Import Execution**\n\nI\'ve made progress! I suspect the error stems from unbundled modules in Vite dev mode, specifically the `formdata-polyfill`. While I couldn\'t find it in the bundled assets, I realize Vite executes unbundled modules directly if imported. So, my next step is to find an import of `formdata-polyfill` in the project.\n\n\n**Preventing Browser Bundling**\n\nI\'ve got it! I realized the issue. Vite is pre-bundling `@google/genai` because it\'s listed in `dependencies`. This pre-bundling includes `node-fetch`, which in turn, brings in `formdata-polyfill`. The `formdata-polyfill` is overwriting `window.fetch` (or trying to)! Since `@google/genai` is server-only, I need to figure out how to stop Vite from bundling it for the browser.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 8
      nanos: 993916575
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
